# My Trello Clone - Project Rules

## About the User
- Not super technical - needs simple, step-by-step instructions
- Prefers bigger tasks split into smaller chunks
- Using this app to organize personal tasks at work and home
- Building this because Trello doesn't allow resizing list widths

## Project Overview
A personal Trello clone with resizable lists. Simpler than Trello but with the key features needed for personal task management.
`README.md` explains how it works; `PROJECT_PLAN.md` lists what's done, known limitations and ideas.

## Features (all built)
- Work and Personal boards, each with an archive; lists can be moved between boards or shared on both
- Lists: create, rename, resize width, minimize, drag to reorder, archive/unarchive
- Cards: create, delete, tick off, drag to reorder or move between lists
- Card details modal with auto-saving title and description
- Search across both boards and archives (highlights matches; doesn't hide non-matches)
- Google sign-in; Row Level Security keeps each user's data private

## Tech Stack
- Next.js 16 with App Router (read `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` for current APIs - see AGENTS.md)
- TypeScript, React 19
- Tailwind CSS v4
- Supabase for database and auth
- @dnd-kit/core and @dnd-kit/sortable for drag and drop
- Playwright for tests

## Code Style
- Use functional components with hooks
- Use TypeScript interfaces for props and data types
- Keep components small and focused (one job per component)
- Use async/await for Supabase operations
- Always handle errors from Supabase queries, and show them to the user (not just console.error)
- Use optimistic UI updates (update UI immediately, then sync with database)
- Use functional state updates (`setLists(prev => ...)`) after any `await`, so changes made in the meantime aren't overwritten

## File Organization
- `app/page.tsx` - main page; only wires hooks and components together
- `app/hooks/` - `useBoard` (loading + all saving), `useBoardDragAndDrop`, `useDragToScroll`
- `app/components/` - UI components
- `app/lib/` - Supabase client, `positions.ts` (ordering), `board.ts` helpers, `sampleData.ts`
- `app/types/` - TypeScript types, including `ListActions` (everything a list can ask the board to do)
- `supabase/schema.sql` - complete current database setup
- `supabase/migrations/` - every database change, oldest first
- `tests/` - Playwright tests and the fake Supabase they use

## Saving Changes (Supabase Patterns)
- All writes go through `enqueueSave(what, save)` in `useBoard.ts`: saves run one at a time in order, and failures
  show a red banner. `what` is a phrase like 'renaming the list' ("Something went wrong renaming the list.")
- Use the `updateCard` / `updateList` / `removeListFromView` helpers in `useBoard.ts` for simple field changes
- Board fetches wait for the save queue, and ignore responses from older fetches
- Use `.select()` to get data back after insert
- Dev bypass mode (`NEXT_PUBLIC_DEV_BYPASS_AUTH=true`) uses demo data and skips all saves

## Ordering (positions)
- `position` columns are fractional (DOUBLE PRECISION). Never renumber a whole list to save a move
- Moving an item: build the final order, call `repositionAfterMove(ordered, movedId)` from `app/lib/positions.ts`,
  apply the returned updates locally and save only those (usually one row)
- New items go at `nextPosition(items)` (highest position + 1), never at `items.length`

## Drag and Drop (@dnd-kit)
- Use DragOverlay for smooth drag visuals (prevents flickering/layout shifts)
- Use closestCorners collision detection; when dragging a list, only consider list droppables
- Prefix list IDs with 'list-' to distinguish from card IDs (e.g., `list-${list.id}`)
- onDragOver only moves a card into a different list (for the live preview); reordering within a list happens in onDragEnd
- onDragEnd works out positions from the snapshot taken at drag start (the database values), not the preview
- onDragCancel restores the snapshot
- Handle both droppable ID formats: 'list-{id}' and raw '{id}' (see `findDropTarget` in `app/lib/board.ts`)

## Styling Guidelines
- Use Tailwind CSS utility classes
- Keep design clean and minimal
- Dark slate background with light "glass" lists and white cards
- Use blue-500/blue-600 for interactive elements
- Make buttons and interactive elements obvious
- Keep UI elements consistent (e.g., delete buttons should look/behave the same everywhere)
- Use hover-to-show for secondary actions (delete buttons appear on hover)
- Use Tailwind group/group-hover for parent-triggered hover effects
- Inputs use 16px text on mobile (`text-base sm:text-sm`) so iOS doesn't zoom in

## Database Tables
- lists: id, user_id, title, position, width, board ('work'/'personal'), shared, archived, minimized, created_at
- cards: id, user_id, list_id, title, description, position, completed, created_at
- Database changes: add a dated file to `supabase/migrations/`, update `supabase/schema.sql` to match,
  and have the user run the SQL in Supabase (SQL Editor) BEFORE pushing code that depends on it

## Communication Style
- Give simple, step-by-step instructions
- Explain what each step does
- Break big tasks into small chunks
- Don't assume technical knowledge
- Be explicit about where to click, what to type, etc.
- When referencing external services (Supabase, GitHub, Vercel), give exact navigation paths
- Run terminal commands directly instead of asking the user to copy-paste
- When something doesn't work, fix it immediately rather than asking for more details

## Git Workflow
- Run `npm test` before committing; all tests must pass
- Commit after each feature is working
- Use clear commit messages: "Add list creation", "Add card drag and drop"
- Push to GitHub after significant progress
- Vercel auto-deploys from GitHub
- **Always tell the user when it's a good time to push** with a reminder like:
 - "Good time to push: [reason]"
 - Suggest the exact commands to run

## Testing Approach
- `npm test` builds the app and runs Playwright tests against a fake Supabase (`tests/fake-supabase.ts`) - no real data is touched
- Add or update a test when adding a feature or fixing a bug
- Also test manually: local (localhost:3000, demo mode) and deployed (Vercel, real data)
- Verify data persists by reloading the page
